\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{A Hölder version on the completion}
\date{2026-11-06}

\p{Fix #{m>0}, and let #{\nu} be the law of \ref{fdip-00BC}.
Let #{\delta} be the shorter-arc metric on the
\vocabk{dyadic index}{fdip-00B6}. The completion of #{D} for
#{\delta} may be identified with the circle
#{\mathbb{R}/\mathbb{Z}} of length #{1}. For every order #{\gamma<1/2}, the coordinate
process of #{\nu} has a version that extends to a random
function on this completion whose sample paths are Hölder
continuous of order #{\gamma}.}

\proof{
\p{The position map sends the class of #{j} at level #{k} to
#{j/2^k} modulo #{1}. It is an isometry of #{\delta} onto the
dyadic rationals in the circle. The level-#{k} points divide
the circle into arcs of length #{2^{-k}}, so every point lies
at distance at most #{2^{-k-1}} from one of them. The dyadic
rationals are dense. The circle is complete, so it is the
completion.}

\p{Fix #{\gamma<1/2}, and choose an integer #{r\ge 2} large
enough that #{(r-1)/(2r)>\gamma}. Cover the circle by 4
closed intervals of length #{1/2}: from #{0} to #{1/2}, from
#{1/4} to #{3/4}, from #{1/2} to #{1}, and from #{3/4} to
#{1/4} through #{0}. On each such interval the shorter arc
between two points equals their distance along the interval.
Parameterize it by a compact interval #{I}. \ref{fdip-00BD} gives
#{\mathbb E\lvert f_s-f_t\rvert^{2r}\le C_r\lvert s-t\rvert^r}
on the dyadic points of #{I}. The Kolmogorov–Chentsov
criterion, in the standard form that builds a modification
from a dyadic grid on a compact interval, produces a version
on those dyadic points which is almost surely Hölder of every
order less than #{(r-1)/(2r)}, and which extends by uniform
continuity to #{I}. In particular the paths are almost surely
Hölder of order #{\gamma} on each of the 4 intervals.}

\p{The 4 almost-sure events may be taken on one common full
set. The index #{D} is countable, so on that set the version
may be chosen to agree with the original coordinates at every
point of #{D}. On each
overlap the two continuous extensions agree on a dense set,
hence agree everywhere. Any two points at shorter arc at most
#{1/4} lie together in one interval of the cover, so there is a
random constant #{H} with
#{\lvert\tilde f_u-\tilde f_v\rvert\le H\delta(u,v)^\gamma}
whenever #{\delta(u,v)\le 1/4}. Paths are bounded, say by a
random #{M}. When #{\delta(u,v)\ge 1/4},
#{\lvert\tilde f_u-\tilde f_v\rvert\le 2M
\le 2M(4\delta(u,v))^\gamma}. The two regimes give a Hölder
bound of order #{\gamma} on the whole circle.}
}
